      answer: "River flooding is managed through hard and soft engineering strategies. Hard engineering: dams (Clywedog Dam on River Severn controls flow but expensive, submerges land); flood walls and embankments (River Thames flood defences, London); channel straightening (increases flow speed but transfers flooding downstream — now less favoured). Soft engineering: floodplain zoning (prohibiting building in flood-prone areas — e.g. Environment Agency planning guidance); afforestation in upper catchments slows runoff (Pickering, North Yorkshire — natural flood management reduced peak flow by 2–3%); managed retreat (allowing coastal or riverside land to flood naturally). The River Severn experiences regular flooding — Shrewsbury was flooded in 2007 and 2020, raising questions about hard engineering cost-effectiveness. The Environment Agency's investment in Somerset Levels (post-2014 flooding) combined dredging with improved pumping stations. Evidence suggests that combining hard and soft approaches is most effective, with soft methods increasingly preferred as more sustainable and cost-effective long-term.",
